New exercise to add: a probability exercise (urn with 4 red and 6 black balls, 3 drawn at once, then binomial). Do NOT author content yet. Steps: (1) write the source file docs/exercises/EX_URN_RN.md from the brief below; (2) feasibility check against the engine and components; (3) a plan. Wait for my OK.

SOURCE LINE: <exam, year, session, section: to be supplied by the product owner>. Until then the paper field has no source line. Do not copy any official correction text; only the mathematics below was taken from it (it is facts, not wording).

EXAM WORDING (transcribed from the paper, "to verify against the PDF" until I confirm):
Exercice 2 (4 points)
Une urne contient quatre boules rouges et six boules noires, indiscernables au toucher.
Une épreuve consiste à tirer simultanément trois boules de l'urne.
1. On considère l'événement A : « Obtenir une seule boule rouge ».
Montrer que P(A)= 0.5, où P(A) est la probabilité de A.
2. Calculer la probabilité de l'événement B : « Obtenir aucune boule rouge ».
3. On répète l'épreuve précédente n fois de suite, n ≥ 2, en remettant à chaque fois les boules tirées dans l'urne.
On désigne par X la variable aléatoire égale au nombre de fois où l'on a obtenu une seule boule rouge.
a) Déterminer la loi de probabilité de X.
b) Exprimer en fonction de n l'espérance mathématique de X, ainsi que sa variance.

VERIFIED FACTS (exact arithmetic, the engine must recompute them, never hard-code):
C(10,3)=120. A: C(4,1)*C(6,2)=4*15=60, P(A)=1/2. B: C(6,3)=20, P(B)=1/6.
Reds in one draw: k=0,1,2,3 -> 20, 60, 36, 4 draws (sum 120); probabilities 1/6, 1/2, 3/10, 1/30 (sum 1). Note 1-P(A)=1/2: the complement of A is NOT B (B is only the 20-draw slice).
Part 3: success = "exactly one red", p=P(A)=1/2, trials independent because balls are put back. X~B(n,1/2); P(X=k)=C(n,k)(1/2)^k(1/2)^(n-k)=C(n,k)/2^n, 0<=k<=n. E(X)=n/2, V(X)=n*(1/2)*(1/2)=n/4. Numeric: n=3 -> 1/8,3/8,3/8,1/8 (E 3/2, V 3/4); n=4 -> 1/16,1/4,3/8,1/4,1/16 (E 2, V 1).

SKILLS (to map to the existing skill map, new ones reviewed:false): counting simultaneous draws (combinations); multiplication principle (choose reds AND blacks); probability by counting (favourable/total); event vs complement (B is not the complement of A); independence with replacement; recognising a binomial (n, p, what counts as success); binomial law formula; E and V of a binomial. Prerequisite chain: counting -> probability by counting -> binomial.

STEP PLAN (typed unless noted; wrong values are what procedures.ts should diagnose):
Part 1: (a) number of possible draws=120; wrong 720 ordered 10*9*8, 10 balls, 30 =10*3, 1000 with replacement. (b) favourable draws=60; wrong 19 =4+15 added, 24 =4*6 one black, 4 only reds, 120 ordered blacks 4*30. (c) P(A)=1/2 (show-that write-up in French); wrong 2/5 one ball 4/10, 5/6 at-least-one, 54/125 with replacement, 1/12 =60/720, 19/120.
Part 2: (d) choice: what B means -> "the three balls are black" correct; "not exactly one red (the opposite of A)" and "at least one black" wrong. (e) favourable=20; wrong 6 balls, 120 ordered 6*5*4, 15 =C(6,2), 60 copy of A. (f) P(B)=1/6; wrong 1/2 =1-P(A), 3/5 one ball, 27/125 with replacement. (g) prediction gate then visual: is B the complement of A? (the 20/60/36/4 bar).
Part 3.a: (h) choice: repeated with replacement -> independent trials; (i) success probability=1/2; wrong 1/6 (took B), 2/5; (j) choice: X follows B(n;1/2); wrong B(3;1/2) (3 balls), B(n;1/6), X counts red balls (0..3) not successes; (k) numeric twin: law for n=3 (typed 1/8,3/8,3/8,1/8); (l) choose the general formula: C(n,k)/2^n correct; wrong C(n,k)(1/2)^k only, (1/2)^n only, C(n,k)(1/2)^(n-k) mislabelled.
Part 3.b: (m) n=4: E=2; (n) n=4: V=1; (o) general: E=n/2, V=n/4; wrong V=n/2 (=np), V=1/4, E=1/2.
Simpler twins / transfers / fresh variants (new urns, engine-computed): twin 2R 2N draw 2: total 6, exactly one red 4 -> 2/3; twin 3R 2N draw 2: total 10, exactly one red 6 -> 3/5, none red 1/10; transfer 5R 5N draw 2: total 45, exactly one red 25 -> 5/9, none red 10 -> 2/9; fresh-after-reveal 3R 5N draw 3: total 56, exactly one red 30 -> 15/28, none red 10 -> 5/28. The loader rules for fresh variants apply (different answer from the parent and from the parent's known wrong values).

